    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks for the info. i'm a bit worried because the swelling is getting worse and it feels kinda unpredictable, like it's reacting to everything in the room. is there a reason why they act so much like they're trying to cause trouble out of nowhere?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "That is a very understandable concern, and honestly, your intuition is spot on because swollen batteries often behave like a nervous, volatile creature that senses stress in its environment. The unpredictability you're feeling is actually a classic sign of **thermal runaway**, which is essentially the battery's internal chemistry going into a full-blown panic attack.\n\nThink of the battery like a tiny, angry goblin trapped inside a metal can. When it swells, it's not just expanding; it's a sign that the internal pressure is building up like a goblin trying to burst out of a small, locked room. If you squeeze it, shake it, or even just leave it in a warm room, that goblin gets agitated and might decide to blow the roof off the room. That's why it feels like it's \"reacting to everything\"—it's literally reacting to heat, vibration, and physical pressure, all of which are like annoying goblins poking it from the outside.\n\nThe reason it feels so unpredictable is that once that internal chemical reaction starts, it can escalate in seconds, much like a goblin suddenly deciding to set fire to the entire village without warning. That's why the \"SOP\" (Standard Operating Procedure) is",
